"""English: extended material for Stage 2 · Experimental design (part B)."""
from . import LE

LE("petak-jalur-strip-plot",
   [("Model and the expectations of three errors",
     [r"In a strip plot, factor A is applied to horizontal strips and factor B to vertical strips in each block. There are three experimental units: the A strip, the B strip, and their intersection. So there are three errors: block × A, block × B, and block × A × B.",
      r"The expected mean squares show that A is tested against error (a), B against error (b), and A × B against error (c). Error (c) is usually the smallest, so the interaction is tested most precisely, while both main effects are tested with few degrees of freedom."]),
    ("Standard errors of comparisons",
     [r"Comparisons of main effects use their own errors. Cell comparisons (for example two levels of A at the same level of B) involve two errors, so the standard error is a combination and the t value is weighted, as in a split plot."]),
    ("When to choose a strip plot",
     [r"A strip plot suits cases where both factors need large plots or heavy equipment, such as tillage method (tractor strips) and irrigation method (channel strips), or plant spacing and row direction. Its advantages are simple field operation and a precise test of the interaction; its drawback is that both main effects are less precise.",
      r"If the main effect of B matters most, a split plot with B on the subplots is better. If the interaction matters most, a strip plot is a good choice."]),
    ("Analysis with a mixed model",
     [r"For balanced data, the classical ANOVA and a mixed model with random block, block × A, and block × B effects give the same tests. For unbalanced or missing data, use a mixed model (REML) with Satterthwaite or Kenward–Roger degrees of freedom."])],
   [(r"A strip plot with \(a=3\), \(b=4\), \(r=4\). Compute the df for errors (a), (b), and (c).",
     r"Error (a): \(3(2)=6\). Error (b): \(3(3)=9\). Error (c): \(3(2)(3)=18\)."),
    (r"Why is the interaction in a strip plot tested more precisely than the main effects?",
     r"The interaction is tested on the intersection units with error (c), which is usually small and has the most degrees of freedom, while the main effects are tested against the larger strip errors with few degrees of freedom."),
    (r"\(E_a=8\), \(E_c=2\), \(r=3\), \(b=3\). Compute the standard error of the difference between two levels of A at the same level of B.",
     r"\(\sqrt{2[(3-1)2+8]/(3\times3)}=\sqrt{2(12)/9}=\sqrt{2.67}=1.63\)."),
    (r"A researcher wants to test 3 tillage methods and 4 irrigation methods, both needing heavy equipment. Which design fits?",
     r"A strip plot: tillage on horizontal strips and irrigation on vertical strips, so both machines move straight across the block."),
    (r"What is the main difference between the error structure of a strip plot and a two-factor split plot?",
     r"A split plot has two errors (main plot and subplot), while a strip plot has three because both factors are applied to large strips and their intersection forms a third unit.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: strip-plot ANOVA",
                soal="Two irrigation methods (A, horizontal strips) and three varieties (B, vertical strips) in three blocks. Yields (quintals/ha) are in the table; the total SS is 830.5.",
                data=[["Block", "A1B1", "A1B2", "A1B3", "A2B1", "A2B2", "A2B3"], ["1", "40", "46", "50", "48", "55", "60"], ["2", "38", "45", "47", "50", "54", "62"], ["3", "42", "47", "52", "49", "57", "61"]],
                langkah=["Correction factor and SS for blocks, A, and B.",
                         "The three errors from the block × A and block × B two-way tables, and the remainder.",
                         "F test for each effect against its own error."],
                simpul="Irrigation and variety are both significant, while the interaction is not; the variety ranking (B3 > B2 > B1) is the same under both irrigation methods. Note that the irrigation F uses error (a) with only 2 df, so its critical value is high (18.5)."))

LE("rancangan-tersarang-nested",
   [("Model and expected mean squares",
     [r"In a two-stage nested design, B is nested in A: each level of B appears in only one level of A. The model has no main effect of B and no A × B interaction, because B(1) in A1 is not the same as B(1) in A2. What exists is the effect of B within A.",
      r"From the expected mean squares, the F denominator for A depends on the nature of B. If B is random (for example trees sampled at random within an orchard), A is tested against the B(A) MS, because the variation between trees enters the MS for A."]),
    ("Variance components and sample allocation",
     [r"Equating each MS with its expectation gives estimators of the variance components (the method of moments, or ANOVA method). When an estimate is negative, it is usually set to zero or REML is used. The share of each component in the total shows at which level the variation is largest.",
      r"For a fixed budget, a cost–variance formula determines how many samples to take at each level. If the cost per tree is \(c_b\) and per leaf \(c_n\), the optimum number of leaves per tree is given below; the larger the between-tree variance relative to the between-leaf variance, the fewer leaves are needed per tree and the more trees."]),
    ("Unbalanced nested designs",
     [r"When the number of trees per orchard or leaves per tree varies, the coefficients of the expected mean squares are no longer simple and the F tests become approximate. Variance components are best estimated with REML, and tests for fixed effects use Satterthwaite degrees of freedom."]),
    ("Nested and crossed in one experiment",
     [r"Many experiments contain both. For example, varieties (fixed) are crossed with locations (random), while plots are nested in locations. Correctly deciding which factors are crossed and which are nested is the first step and determines the whole analysis; a practical rule: if the level labels of a factor mean the same thing at every level of another factor, the factors are crossed."])],
   [(r"In a nested design, why is there no A × B interaction?",
     r"Because each level of B appears in only one level of A; B(1) in orchard 1 and B(1) in orchard 2 are different trees, so there is no common effect of B to compare across levels of A."),
    (r"\(a=4\) locations, \(b=3\) plots per location, \(n=2\) samples per plot. Compute the df for A, B(A), and error.",
     r"A: 3. B(A): \(4(3-1)=8\). Error: \(12(2-1)=12\). Total: \(24-1=23\)."),
    (r"MSA \(=60\), MS B(A) \(=12\), MSE \(=4\), \(b=3\), \(n=2\). Estimate the three variance components.",
     r"\(\hat{\sigma}^2=4\); \(\hat{\sigma}^2_\beta=(12-4)/2=4\); \(\hat{\sigma}^2_\alpha=(60-12)/6=8\)."),
    (r"The cost of examining one tree is \(c_b=20\) and one leaf \(c_n=1\), with \(\sigma^2=0.5\) (between leaves) and \(\sigma^2_\beta=2\) (between trees). What is the optimum number of leaves per tree?",
     r"\(n_{\text{opt}}=\sqrt{20(0.5)/(1\times2)}=\sqrt{5}=2.2\), rounded to 2 leaves per tree."),
    (r"The same varieties are grown at several locations, and each location has several plots. Which factors are crossed and which are nested?",
     r"Varieties are crossed with locations (the same varieties are at every location); plots are nested in locations (plot 1 at location A is not plot 1 at location B).")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: nested ANOVA and variance components",
                soal="Leaf nitrogen was measured in 3 orchards, 3 trees per orchard (random), and 2 leaves per tree. Data are in the table; the total SS is 71.11.",
                data=[["Orchard", "Tree 1", "Tree 2", "Tree 3", "Total"], ["1", "10, 11", "12, 13", "9, 10", "65"], ["2", "14, 15", "16, 15", "13, 14", "87"], ["3", "11, 12", "10, 10", "12, 13", "68"]],
                langkah=["Orchard SS from the orchard totals.",
                         "SS for trees within orchards from the tree totals (21, 25, 19; 29, 31, 27; 23, 20, 25).",
                         "Error SS (between leaves) and the mean squares.",
                         "F tests: orchards against MS B(A), trees against MSE.",
                         "Variance components."],
                simpul="About 65% of the variation comes from differences between orchards, 27% between trees within orchards, and only 8% between leaves within a tree. For the next survey, adding orchards is far more useful than adding leaves per tree."))

LE("pengukuran-berulang-repeated-measures",
   [("Sphericity and the epsilon correction",
     [r"The within-subject F test in RM-ANOVA is valid when the covariance matrix satisfies sphericity: the variance of the difference between any two times is the same. The departure is measured by the Greenhouse–Geisser epsilon \(\hat{\varepsilon}\), computed from the covariance matrix of orthonormal contrasts between times. It ranges from \(1/(k-1)\) (worst departure) to 1 (perfect sphericity).",
      r"The correction multiplies both degrees of freedom of the F test by \(\hat{\varepsilon}\). The Greenhouse–Geisser correction tends to be conservative when \(\varepsilon\) is near 1; the Huynh–Feldt correction is more liberal. A practical guide: use Huynh–Feldt when \(\hat{\varepsilon}>0.75\), Greenhouse–Geisser when it is smaller."]),
    ("The multivariate approach",
     [r"An alternative without the sphericity assumption is multivariate analysis (repeated-measures MANOVA): the differences between times are treated as a response vector and tested with Hotelling's \(T^2\). This approach does not require sphericity, but it needs more subjects than time points and has low power when subjects are few."]),
    ("Mixed models and covariance structures",
     [r"A mixed model models the correlation between times directly by choosing a covariance structure: compound symmetry (equivalent to RM-ANOVA with sphericity), first-order autoregressive (correlation decaying with time lag, \(\rho^{|t-t'|}\)), or unstructured (every variance and covariance free). The structure is chosen by AIC or BIC.",
      r"The main advantages of mixed models: subjects with incomplete data are still used, measurement times need not be uniform, and time-varying covariates can be included."]),
    ("Time contrasts and profile analysis",
     [r"For ordered times, the time effect is often more meaningfully broken down with polynomial contrasts (linear, quadratic trends) than an overall test. In designs with treatment groups, the main question is usually the group × time interaction: whether the profiles of change differ between groups."])],
   [(r"Why does the Greenhouse–Geisser correction change only the P-value and not the F value?",
     r"The correction multiplies the numerator and denominator degrees of freedom by \(\hat{\varepsilon}\). The F value stays the same, but its reference distribution has fewer df, so the P-value increases."),
    (r"In an RM-ANOVA with \(k=4\) times and \(n=12\) subjects, the time F \(=5.0\) and \(\hat{\varepsilon}=0.6\). What are the df after correction?",
     r"Before correction: 3 and 33. After correction: \(0.6\times3=1.8\) and \(0.6\times33=19.8\)."),
    (r"What is the lower bound of \(\varepsilon\) for \(k=5\) times?",
     r"\(1/(k-1)=1/4=0.25\)."),
    (r"Measurements were taken on days 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and some subjects are missing day 16. Which method is appropriate?",
     r"A mixed model with a suitable covariance structure (for example AR(1) using the actual time lags, or unstructured). Classical RM-ANOVA discards subjects with incomplete data."),
    (r"In an experiment with two treatment groups measured at 4 times, which effect is most important to test?",
     r"The group × time interaction, because it shows whether the pattern of change over time differs between treatments.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: one-way within-subject RM-ANOVA",
                soal="Five subjects were measured at three times (weeks 0, 2, 4). Data are in the table; the total SS is 148.93.",
                data=[["Subject", "W0", "W2", "W4", "Total"], ["1", "10", "14", "15", "39"], ["2", "12", "15", "19", "46"], ["3", "9", "11", "13", "33"], ["4", "11", "16", "18", "45"], ["5", "13", "15", "20", "48"], ["Mean", "11.0", "14.2", "17.0", ""]],
                langkah=["Subject SS from the subject totals (between subjects, removed from the error).",
                         "Time SS from the time totals (55, 71, 85).",
                         "Error (subject × time) with df (5 − 1)(3 − 1) = 8.",
                         "F test for time.",
                         "Greenhouse–Geisser correction from the covariance matrix between times."],
                simpul="Time has a highly significant effect; the mean rises from 11.0 to 17.0. An epsilon of 0.93 shows sphericity is nearly met. If subjects were ignored (ordinary ANOVA), the subject SS of 50.27 would go into the error and F would drop to 90.13/2 ÷ 58.80/12 = 9.2."))

LE("rancangan-cross-over",
   [("The 2×2 crossover model",
     [r"In an AB/BA crossover, each subject receives both treatments in two periods. The model contains a subject effect (random), period, treatment, and possibly a carry-over effect from the first-period treatment. With the period difference per subject \(d=y_1-y_2\), the subject effect disappears and the comparison is made between sequences."]),
    ("The problem with the two-stage test",
     [r"The old procedure (Grizzle) first tested carry-over from the sum of the two periods per subject; if significant, only the first period was used. Freeman (1989) showed that this two-stage procedure seriously inflates the type I error, because the carry-over test is based on a weak between-subject comparison.",
      r"Current guidance: prevent carry-over with an adequate washout period based on biological knowledge, rather than relying on a statistical test to detect it."]),
    ("Crossover designs with more than two periods",
     [r"Designs with three or more periods (for example ABB/BAA) or more than two treatments (Williams Latin squares) allow treatment effects to be estimated validly even in the presence of first-order carry-over. A Williams square is arranged so that each treatment is preceded by every other treatment exactly once."]),
    ("Sample size and efficiency",
     [r"Because comparisons are made within subjects, the relevant variance is the within-subject error variance, not the total variance. When the correlation between periods on the same subject is \(\rho\), a crossover needs roughly \((1-\rho)\) times as many observations as a parallel design for the same precision (counted in total observations)."])],
   [(r"In a 2×2 crossover, the mean period difference is 3.0 for sequence AB and −1.0 for sequence BA. Estimate the treatment effect A − B and the period effect.",
     r"\(\hat{\tau}_A-\hat{\tau}_B=(3.0-(-1.0))/2=2.0\). Period effect (period 1 − period 2) \(=(3.0+(-1.0))/2=1.0\)."),
    (r"Why is the carry-over test in a 2×2 crossover weak?",
     r"Because it uses the sum of the two periods per subject compared between sequences, so it contains the large between-subject variation."),
    (r"What is the key property of a Williams Latin square for 4 treatments?",
     r"Each treatment appears once in each period, and each treatment is preceded by every other treatment exactly once, so first-order carry-over effects are balanced."),
    (r"The correlation between periods is \(\rho=0.6\). A parallel design needs 80 subjects. Roughly how many subjects does a 2×2 crossover need?",
     r"\(80\times(1-0.6)/2=16\) subjects."),
    (r"When is a crossover unsuitable?",
     r"When the subject's condition changes permanently (a disease is cured, an animal grows quickly), when treatment effects cannot wash out, or when the response is a one-time event (such as death).")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: analysing a 2×2 crossover",
                soal="Eight cows received feeds A and B; four in sequence AB and four in BA, with a washout period. Milk yield (L/day) is in the table.",
                data=[["Sequence", "Cow", "Period 1", "Period 2", "d = P1 − P2", "Sum"], ["AB", "1", "12", "9", "3", "21"], ["AB", "2", "14", "10", "4", "24"], ["AB", "3", "11", "9", "2", "20"], ["AB", "4", "13", "11", "2", "24"], ["BA", "5", "8", "11", "−3", "19"], ["BA", "6", "10", "12", "−2", "22"], ["BA", "7", "9", "13", "−4", "22"], ["BA", "8", "9", "12", "−3", "21"]],
                langkah=["Mean period difference by sequence.",
                         "Treatment effect and period effect.",
                         "Pooled standard deviation of the differences and the standard error of the treatment effect.",
                         "t test with 6 df.",
                         "Carry-over check from the sum per cow (means 22.25 and 21.0)."],
                simpul="Feed A gives 2.9 L/day more than B (P = 0.0001), with no meaningful period effect. There is no sign of carry-over, but remember this test is weak; the validity of the analysis rests mainly on an adequate washout period."))

LE("ancova-analisis-kovarians",
   [("Deriving the coefficient and adjusted means",
     [r"One-way ANCOVA models the response as a treatment effect plus a regression on the covariate with the same slope in every treatment. The common slope is estimated from the within-treatment sums of squares and products (the error component \(E\)), so it is not affected by differences between treatments.",
      r"The adjusted mean is the predicted response of each treatment at the same covariate value, namely the grand covariate mean. Its standard error increases when a treatment's covariate mean is far from the grand mean."]),
    ("Adjusted sums of squares",
     [r"The adjusted error SS is the remainder after the within-treatment regression; the adjusted treatment SS is the difference between the adjusted total SS and the adjusted error SS, because treatments and the covariate are not orthogonal. The error degrees of freedom are reduced by one for the slope."]),
    ("Requirements for a valid covariate",
     [r"The covariate must be measured before the treatment is applied or be impossible to affect by the treatment (initial weight, age, initial soil nutrients). If the covariate is affected by the treatment (for example plant height in mid-experiment), adjusting for it removes part of the treatment effect and gives misleading results.",
      r"In addition, the response–covariate relationship must be linear with equal slopes across treatments; the treatment × covariate interaction test checks the last requirement."]),
    ("The gain in precision",
     [r"When the within-treatment correlation between response and covariate is \(\rho\), the error variance falls to roughly \((1-\rho^2)\) times its original value. With \(\rho=0.7\) the error variance is cut by about half, equivalent to doubling the replication. ANCOVA is often cheaper than adding replicates, provided the covariate is easy to measure."])],
   [(r"Why is the ANCOVA slope estimated from the within-treatment component, not from all the data?",
     r"So that differences between treatments do not influence the slope. If it were computed from all the data, treatment effects related to the covariate means would be mixed up with the response–covariate relationship."),
    (r"Treatment A has a mean response of 50 with a covariate mean of 22; the grand covariate mean is 20; \(\hat{\beta}=1.5\). Compute the adjusted mean of A.",
     r"\(50-1.5(22-20)=47.0\)."),
    (r"Plant height at week 4 is used as a covariate for yield in a fertiliser experiment. What is the problem?",
     r"Height at week 4 is already affected by the fertiliser, so adjusting for it removes part of the fertiliser effect. The covariate must be measured before treatment or be unaffected by it."),
    (r"The within-treatment correlation between initial and final weight is 0.8. Roughly what percentage of the error variance is removed by ANCOVA?",
     r"\(1-\rho^2=0.36\), so the error variance falls by about 64%."),
    (r"The treatment × covariate interaction test is significant. What does that mean for ANCOVA?",
     r"The slopes differ between treatments, so treatment differences depend on the covariate value. A single adjusted mean is misleading; report comparisons at several covariate values or fit separate slopes.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: a complete ANCOVA",
                soal="Three feeds were tested on 12 animals (4 per feed). Initial weight x and final weight y (kg) are in the table.",
                data=[["Feed", "x (initial)", "y (final)", "Mean x", "Mean y"], ["F1", "20, 22, 24, 26", "40, 43, 45, 48", "23", "44.00"], ["F2", "21, 23, 25, 27", "44, 47, 48, 52", "24", "47.75"], ["F3", "22, 24, 26, 28", "50, 52, 55, 57", "25", "53.50"]],
                langkah=["Within-treatment (error) and total sums of squares and products.",
                         "Common slope.",
                         "Adjusted error SS (df 12 − 3 − 1 = 8) and adjusted treatment SS.",
                         "Adjusted F test with df 2 and 8.",
                         "Adjusted means at the grand mean x = 24."],
                simpul="Without the covariate, the MSE is 10.64 and the feed F is only 8.6. Adjusting for initial weight cuts the MSE to 0.25 and makes the feed differences much clearer. The adjusted means (45.25, 47.75, 52.25) are closer together than the raw means because F3 happened to get heavier animals."))

LE("manova",
   [("The H and E matrices",
     [r"MANOVA extends the partition of sums of squares to matrices: the hypothesis matrix \(\mathbf{H}\) (sums of squares and products between groups) and the error matrix \(\mathbf{E}\) (within groups). Their diagonals are the univariate SS of each response, and the off-diagonal elements are sums of products that capture the correlation between responses.",
      r"All test statistics are built from the eigenvalues of \(\mathbf{E}^{-1}\mathbf{H}\), which describe the ratio of between-group to within-group variation along the combinations of responses that discriminate most."]),
    ("Two groups: Hotelling's T²",
     [r"For two groups, MANOVA is equivalent to Hotelling's \(T^2\) test, the multivariate extension of the t test. It uses the pooled covariance matrix and converts exactly to an F distribution. All four MANOVA statistics give the same conclusion because there is only one nonzero eigenvalue."]),
    ("Choosing a test statistic",
     [r"When the group differences lie along one direction (one dominant eigenvalue), Roy's statistic is the most powerful. When they are spread over several directions, Pillai's is the most powerful and the most robust to violated assumptions, especially unequal covariance matrices with unequal group sizes. So Pillai's is often recommended as the default."]),
    ("Why ANOVA per response is not enough",
     [r"MANOVA can detect differences invisible in the ANOVA of each response, when groups differ on a combination of responses (for example a ratio of two measurements) while each response on its own overlaps. Conversely, when responses are nearly uncorrelated and only one differs, MANOVA can be less powerful than univariate ANOVA. Choose according to the research question: do the responses form one joint concept?"])],
   [(r"The two eigenvalues of \(\mathbf{E}^{-1}\mathbf{H}\) are 1.0 and 0.25. Compute Wilks' lambda and Pillai's trace.",
     r"\(\Lambda=\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{1.25}=0.40\). \(V=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{0.25}{1.25}=0.5+0.2=0.70\)."),
    (r"For two groups with \(n_1=n_2=10\) and \(p=3\) responses, \(T^2=12\). Convert it to an F statistic.",
     r"\(F=\frac{20-3-1}{3(18)}\times12=\frac{16}{54}\times12=3.56\), with df 3 and 16."),
    (r"Why is Pillai's trace recommended as the default statistic?",
     r"It is the most robust to violated assumptions, especially unequal covariance matrices with unequal group sizes, and reasonably powerful when differences are spread over several directions."),
    (r"How can MANOVA be significant when the ANOVA of each of two responses is not?",
     r"When groups differ on a combination of two correlated responses, for example along a direction perpendicular to the overall correlation, so each response on its own overlaps but the combination separates them clearly."),
    (r"After a significant MANOVA with 4 responses, what follow-up is usual?",
     r"ANOVA for each response with a Bonferroni correction (α/4), or discriminant analysis to find the combination of responses that best separates the groups.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: Hotelling's T² for two groups",
                soal="Two varieties were measured on two traits: panicle length (y₁) and 100-grain weight (y₂), 4 plants each. Data are in the table.",
                data=[["Variety", "y₁", "y₂"], ["V1", "5, 6, 7, 6", "8, 9, 9, 10"], ["V2", "8, 9, 8, 9", "9, 11, 10, 12"], ["Mean V1", "6.0", "9.0"], ["Mean V2", "8.5", "10.5"]],
                langkah=["Pooled covariance matrix.",
                         "Difference of the mean vectors.",
                         "The T² statistic.",
                         "Convert to F with df 2 and 5.",
                         "Compare with univariate t tests."],
                simpul="The two varieties differ multivariately (P = 0.013). 100-grain weight alone is not significant, but because it is correlated with panicle length, its information strengthens the separation of the two varieties."))

LE("response-surface-methodology-rsm",
   [("A sequential strategy: from the slope to the peak",
     [r"RSM usually proceeds in stages. When the experiment is still far from the optimum, a first-order model is fitted and steepest ascent is carried out: runs are made along the gradient direction \((b_1,b_2,\dots)\) until the response stops increasing. Only around that point is a second-order design run to model the curvature.",
      r"A curvature test from centre points in a factorial design shows when to switch: if the mean of the centre points differs clearly from the mean of the factorial points, the surface is already curving."]),
    ("Central composite and Box–Behnken designs",
     [r"A central composite design (CCD) consists of \(2^k\) factorial points, axial points at distance \(\alpha\) from the centre, and several centre points. With \(\alpha=(2^k)^{1/4}\) the design is rotatable: the prediction variance depends only on the distance from the centre. Replicated centre points provide a pure-error estimate for the lack-of-fit test.",
      r"A Box–Behnken design uses points at the midpoints of the cube edges, with no corner points. It suits cases where the extreme combination of all factors is dangerous or impossible to run."]),
    ("Canonical analysis",
     [r"The stationary point is found by setting the gradient of the second-order model to zero. To judge its type, the model is rewritten in canonical axes centred at the stationary point and rotated by the eigenvectors of \(\mathbf{B}\). A very small eigenvalue means the response is nearly flat in that direction (a ridge), giving freedom to choose a cheaper factor combination without losing response."]),
    ("Lack of fit and validation",
     [r"The error SS is split into pure error (from replicated centre points) and lack of fit. Significant lack of fit means the second-order model is inadequate; a response transformation or a higher-order model should be tried. Once the optimum is found, run several confirmation runs at that point and compare them with the prediction interval."])],
   [(r"A first-order model gives \(\hat{y}=50+4x_1+2x_2\). If the step in \(x_1\) is 1 coded unit, what is the step in \(x_2\) along the path of steepest ascent?",
     r"Proportional to the coefficients: \(\Delta x_2=1\times2/4=0.5\) coded units."),
    (r"How many runs does a CCD for \(k=3\) factors with 6 centre points need, and what \(\alpha\) is rotatable?",
     r"\(N=8+6+6=20\). \(\alpha=8^{1/4}=1.682\)."),
    (r"The eigenvalues of \(\mathbf{B}\) are −2.1 and 0.9. What type of stationary point is it?",
     r"A saddle point, because the eigenvalues have different signs; the response rises in one direction and falls in another."),
    (r"The eigenvalues are −3.2 and −0.05. What does this mean in practice?",
     r"A maximum with a nearly flat ridge in the direction of the −0.05 eigenvalue. The factors can be moved along that direction (for example to a cheaper combination) with almost no loss of response."),
    (r"Why should centre points in a CCD be replicated?",
     r"To estimate pure error for the lack-of-fit test and to stabilise the prediction variance near the centre.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: a two-factor CCD and its optimum",
                soal="Temperature (x₁) and time (x₂) in coded units were run in a rotatable CCD (α = 1.414) with 5 centre points. The yield response (%) is in the table.",
                data=[["x₁", "x₂", "y"], ["−1", "−1", "76.5"], ["1", "−1", "78.0"], ["−1", "1", "77.0"], ["1", "1", "79.5"], ["−1.414", "0", "75.6"], ["1.414", "0", "78.4"], ["0", "−1.414", "77.0"], ["0", "1.414", "78.5"], ["0 (×5)", "0", "79.9; 80.3; 80.0; 79.7; 79.8"]],
                langkah=["Second-order model by least squares.",
                         "The B matrix and the stationary point.",
                         "Eigenvalues of B (both negative means a maximum).",
                         "Lack-of-fit test: pure error from the 5 centre points."],
                simpul="The optimum lies slightly above the centre (x₁ = 0.39; x₂ = 0.31 in coded units) with a predicted yield of 80.2%. The second-order model is adequate (lack of fit not significant). Convert back to natural units and run confirmation runs at that point."))

LE("rancangan-campuran-mixture",
   [("Why a mixture model has no intercept",
     [r"Because \(x_1+x_2+\dots+x_q=1\), the intercept column equals the sum of the component columns, and a pure quadratic term \(x_i^2\) can be written as \(x_i(1-\sum_{j\ne i}x_j)\). The Scheffé model absorbs this dependency: the intercept and pure quadratic terms are dropped, and the coefficients have a direct interpretation for pure components and binary blends."]),
    ("Interpreting binary coefficients",
     [r"For a 50:50 blend of components \(i\) and \(j\), the quadratic model predicts the average of the two pure components plus \(\beta_{ij}/4\). So \(\beta_{ij}\) measures the departure of the blend from a straight line (linear blending): positive means synergism, negative antagonism. The largest excess occurs at the 50:50 point."]),
    ("Lower bounds and pseudo-components",
     [r"In practice components are often constrained, for example each at least 10%. The experimental region becomes a smaller simplex, and proportions are converted to pseudo-components so the design and model keep the standard simplex form. When there are upper bounds too, the region is no longer a simplex and points are chosen by an optimality criterion (for example a D-optimal design)."]),
    ("Mixtures with process variables",
     [r"When process factors (temperature, processing time) are present besides proportions, the model combines the mixture model with the process factors, usually by multiplying mixture terms by process terms. The number of parameters grows quickly, so the design needs careful planning."])],
   [(r"A three-component quadratic model: \(\beta_1=10\), \(\beta_2=12\), \(\beta_3=8\), \(\beta_{12}=6\), \(\beta_{13}=-4\), \(\beta_{23}=0\). Predict the 50:50 blend of components 1 and 2.",
     r"\((10+12)/2+6/4=11+1.5=12.5\)."),
    (r"With the same model, what does \(\beta_{13}=-4\) mean?",
     r"Components 1 and 3 are antagonistic: their blend gives a lower response than the linear average of the pure components, at most 1 unit below the line at the 50:50 point."),
    (r"How many points does a simplex-centroid design for 3 components have?",
     r"\(2^3-1=7\): three pure points, three 50:50 binary blends, and one centroid (1/3, 1/3, 1/3)."),
    (r"Component 1 is at least 0.2 and component 2 at least 0.1, with no bound on component 3. Compute the pseudo-components for the blend (0.5, 0.3, 0.2).",
     r"\(1-\sum L=0.7\). \(x_1'=(0.5-0.2)/0.7=0.429\), \(x_2'=(0.3-0.1)/0.7=0.286\), \(x_3'=0.2/0.7=0.286\)."),
    (r"Why is an ordinary factorial design unsuitable for a formulation experiment?",
     r"Because proportions cannot change freely: raising one component necessarily lowers another. A factorial design treats factors as independent and would produce combinations whose sum is not 1.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: a Scheffé model from a simplex-centroid",
                soal="Three ingredients (x₁, x₂, x₃) were tested with a simplex-centroid design for product hardness. Data are in the table.",
                data=[["x₁", "x₂", "x₃", "y"], ["1", "0", "0", "11.0"], ["0", "1", "0", "8.0"], ["0", "0", "1", "14.0"], ["½", "½", "0", "13.5"], ["½", "0", "½", "15.0"], ["0", "½", "½", "9.5"], ["⅓", "⅓", "⅓", "13.0"]],
                langkah=["Linear coefficients from least squares, almost equal to the pure-component responses.",
                         "Binary coefficients.",
                         "Check the 50:50 blend of components 1 and 2.",
                         "The centroid serves as a check on the fit of the quadratic model."],
                simpul="Ingredients 1 and 2 are strongly synergistic (β₁₂ = 15.7), 1 and 3 are also synergistic (9.7), while 2 and 3 are antagonistic (−6.3). The centroid residual is small, so the quadratic model is adequate; the best formulation lies around a blend of ingredients 1 and 3."))

LE("metode-taguchi",
   [("Control factors and noise factors",
     [r"The core idea of Taguchi is robust design: choosing control-factor levels so that the response stays good even when noise factors (hard or expensive to control in production) vary. Control factors go in the inner array and noise factors in the outer array; each row of the inner array is run under all conditions of the outer array.",
      r"The SN ratio summarises the mean and variability of the response over the noise conditions in one number; the level that maximises SN is the robust level."]),
    ("Orthogonal arrays and linear graphs",
     [r"Orthogonal arrays are low-resolution fractional factorials in Taguchi's notation: \(L_4(2^3)\), \(L_8(2^7)\), \(L_9(3^4)\), \(L_{18}(2^1\times3^7)\). Every pair of columns is balanced. When a particular interaction must be estimated, linear graphs or interaction tables show which column must be left empty to hold it."]),
    ("Criticism and the response-model approach",
     [r"Statisticians (Box, Nair, and others) criticised several aspects: the SN ratio mixes the mean and the variance so their effects are hard to separate, resolution III arrays can mislead when interactions exist, and “pick the best level” analysis without formal tests is prone to spurious effects.",
      r"An alternative models the mean and the log standard deviation separately, or includes noise factors directly in the response model (the response model approach) so that control × noise interactions, the real source of robustness, can be estimated explicitly."]),
    ("Prediction and confirmation",
     [r"After the optimum levels are chosen, the SN at that combination is predicted with an additive model from the effects of the influential factors. A confirmation run at the optimum combination is essential; if its result is far from the prediction, there is probably an ignored interaction."])],
   [(r"A smaller-the-better response (surface roughness) is observed as 2, 3, and 2 under three noise conditions. Compute the smaller-the-better SN.",
     r"\(\frac13(4+9+4)=5.667\); \(SN=-10\log_{10}5.667=-7.53\) dB."),
    (r"What is the maximum number of two-level factors that fit in an \(L_8\)?",
     r"Seven factors (\(L_8(2^7)\)), if no interactions are estimated."),
    (r"What is the main criticism of the nominal-the-best SN ratio?",
     r"It mixes the mean and the variance into one number, so factors that shift the mean and factors that change the variance are hard to distinguish; it is clearer to model the mean and the log standard deviation separately."),
    (r"The overall mean SN is 27.8 dB. Factor A at its best level gives 28.5 dB, factor C at its best level 28.5 dB, and factor B has no effect. Predict the optimum SN.",
     r"\(27.8+(28.5-27.8)+(28.5-27.8)=29.2\) dB."),
    (r"The confirmation run is far below the predicted SN. What could be the cause?",
     r"An interaction among control factors aliased with main effects in the orthogonal array, or a new noise factor not covered by the outer array. The additive model no longer holds.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: an L4 with two noise conditions",
                soal="Three two-level control factors (A, B, C) were placed in an L4(2³). Each row was run under two noise conditions; the response is larger-the-better (tensile strength). Data are in the table.",
                data=[["Run", "A", "B", "C", "y (N1, N2)", "SN (dB)"], ["1", "1", "1", "1", "20, 22", "26.41"], ["2", "1", "2", "2", "25, 24", "27.78"], ["3", "2", "1", "2", "28, 30", "29.23"], ["4", "2", "2", "1", "26, 23", "27.73"]],
                langkah=["Larger-the-better SN for run 1.",
                         "Mean SN at each factor level.",
                         "Choose the level with the highest SN and predict the optimum SN (B ignored)."],
                simpul="The robust combination is A2 and C2; B has almost no effect, so its level can be chosen on cost. The combination A2B1C2 happens to be run 3 (SN 29.23), matching the prediction of 29.20; still carry out a confirmation with fresh replicates."))
